  Cell[
   "7. Analytical Discussion and Quantitative Insights",
   "Section"
  ],
  Cell[
   "(A) Why ARMA Models Excel at Expected Average Price Prediction",
   "Subsection"
  ],
  Cell[
   "1. Mean Reversion to Unconditional Drift: In liquid equities, daily returns mean-revert rapidly to the long-term equity risk premium drift (mu_r approx +0.09%/day for SPY). ARMA forecasts converge to this drift, providing a grounded multi-step price expectation that does not explode.",
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   "2. Noise Absorption: Transitory daily noise shocks are absorbed by the moving average (MA) polynomial, preventing transient market volatility from distorting multi-week valuation expectations.",
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   "3. Across 16 rolling windows, ARMA achieved a 1.64% MAPE on average future price, outperforming both the Naive benchmark (2.10%) and the pure linear Kalman trend (2.62%). ",
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   "(B) The Strengths and Limitations of the Kalman Filter",
   "Subsection"
  ],
  Cell[
   "1. Zero Phase Lag in Dynamic Trend Extraction: Unlike simple moving averages (which lag price changes by half their window width, e.g. ~10 days for a 20-day SMA), the Kalman filter level mu_t dynamically tracks true shifts with minimal delay while attenuating high-frequency noise.",
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   "2. Momentum Quantification: The slope state beta_t ($/day) provides an instantaneous measure of trend direction and acceleration.",
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   TextData[
    {
     "3. Extrapolation Risk (Momentum Overshoot): Linear extrapolation (P",
     StyleBox["{T+h} = mu", FontSlant -> Italic],
     "T + h * beta_T) assumes uninhibited momentum continuation. If a stock enters the forecast window on a sharp short-term rally, the Kalman filter projects continued linear growth, resulting in overshooting if the market consolidates or pulls back. "
    }
   ],
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   "(C) Quantitative Finance Architecture Recommendation",
   "Subsection"
  ],
  Cell[
   TextData[
    {
     "1. In-Sample Signal Generation: Use the Kalman Filter to extract zero-lag trends (mu",
     StyleBox[
      "t) and instantaneous momentum (beta",
      FontSlant -> Italic
     ],
     "t) for regime detection and trade entry/exit triggers."
    }
   ],
   "Text"
  ],
  Cell[
   TextData[
    {
     "2. Forward Valuation & Horizon Expectation: Use ARMA on returns (or a Damped-Trend Kalman Filter where beta",
     StyleBox["{t+1} = phi * beta", FontSlant -> Italic],
     "t with 0 < phi < 1) when computing expected average future prices, forward strike targets, or risk limits over multi-week horizons."
    }
   ],
   "Text"
  ]
 },
